1
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:06,906
Module one: cardiac arrest and the Chain

2
00:00:06,906 --> 00:00:09,839
of Survival. Each heartbeat begins as an

3
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:11,898
electrical impulse that spreads through

4
00:00:11,898 --> 00:00:14,101
the muscle, so the atria and then the

5
00:00:14,101 --> 00:00:16,753
ventricles contract in sequence, pushing

6
00:00:16,753 --> 00:00:18,116
blood out to the body.

7
00:00:20,204 --> 00:00:23,210
Cardiac arrest is an electrical problem.

8
00:00:23,498 --> 00:00:26,210
In ventricular fibrillation, the chambers

9
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:27,655
lose their coordination:

10
00:00:27,655 --> 00:00:30,121
muscle fibres fire out of step, and the

11
00:00:30,121 --> 00:00:32,889
ventricles quiver instead of contracting.

12
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:35,849
A quivering ventricle ejects no blood, so

13
00:00:35,849 --> 00:00:38,640
cardiac output falls to zero and the pulse

14
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,319
is lost within seconds.

15
00:00:40,598 --> 00:00:43,016
Without blood to the brain, the patient

16
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:44,765
loses consciousness.

17
00:00:45,022 --> 00:00:47,279
Occasional gasping is not normal

18
00:00:47,279 --> 00:00:50,500
breathing. This patient is unresponsive,

19
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:51,689
with no pulse.

20
00:00:53,854 --> 00:00:56,342
A heart attack is a different problem.

21
00:00:56,594 --> 00:00:59,187
The coronary arteries on the surface of

22
00:00:59,187 --> 00:01:01,763
the heart supply its muscle with blood.

23
00:01:01,928 --> 00:01:04,527
In a heart attack, one is blocked, and the

24
00:01:04,527 --> 00:01:06,967
part of the muscle it supplies is starved

25
00:01:06,967 --> 00:01:10,164
of blood. That is a circulation problem,

26
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:12,543
and the patient is usually conscious,

27
00:01:12,543 --> 00:01:14,119
often with chest pain.

28
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,029
A heart attack can trigger cardiac arrest,

29
00:01:17,029 --> 00:01:19,301
but arrest itself is the electrical

30
00:01:19,301 --> 00:01:20,999
failure of the whole pump.

31
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:25,672
Cardiac arrest has four rhythms, and the

32
00:01:25,672 --> 00:01:27,736
rhythm decides the treatment.

33
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,118
Two are shockable:

34
00:01:29,118 --> 00:01:31,744
ventricular fibrillation, and pulseless

35
00:01:31,744 --> 00:01:33,872
ventricular tachycardia, where the

36
00:01:33,872 --> 00:01:36,607
ventricles beat so fast they cannot fill.

37
00:01:36,806 --> 00:01:39,664
A shock depolarises the muscle at once,

38
00:01:39,664 --> 00:01:42,104
letting the heart's own pacemaker restore

39
00:01:42,104 --> 00:01:43,637
an organised rhythm.

40
00:01:43,796 --> 00:01:45,736
The other two are not shockable:

41
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:48,440
pulseless electrical activity, organised

42
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:51,067
electrical activity without a pulse, and

43
00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:53,722
asystole, no electrical activity.

44
00:01:53,954 --> 00:01:56,333
These are treated with cardiopulmonary

45
00:01:56,333 --> 00:01:58,316
resuscitation and the search for

46
00:01:58,316 --> 00:01:59,757
reversible causes.

47
00:02:01,954 --> 00:02:04,289
How often do patients survive?

48
00:02:04,502 --> 00:02:07,933
About 350,000 people a year in the United

49
00:02:07,933 --> 00:02:10,282
States have an out-of-hospital cardiac

50
00:02:10,282 --> 00:02:13,328
arrest, and it results in death about 90%

51
00:02:13,328 --> 00:02:16,077
of the time. Survival to discharge is

52
00:02:16,077 --> 00:02:20,948
approximately 10.5% overall, 21.1% in a

53
00:02:20,948 --> 00:02:24,095
public place, and 8.9% at home, where

54
00:02:24,095 --> 00:02:25,976
fewer arrests are witnessed.

55
00:02:26,138 --> 00:02:30,012
In hospital it is 23.6% in adults and

56
00:02:30,012 --> 00:02:33,738
45.2% in children, because help is close

57
00:02:33,738 --> 00:02:37,892
by. Yet only about 41% receive bystander

58
00:02:37,892 --> 00:02:40,212
compressions before help arrives.

59
00:02:42,237 --> 00:02:45,091
That is why the nearest person matters.

60
00:02:45,485 --> 00:02:47,391
Chest compressions squeeze the heart

61
00:02:47,391 --> 00:02:49,733
between the sternum and the spine, pushing

62
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:51,879
blood to the brain and the heart muscle,

63
00:02:51,879 --> 00:02:53,984
and they keep circulation going until the

64
00:02:53,984 --> 00:02:55,468
heart is restarted.

65
00:02:55,787 --> 00:02:58,460
Given immediately, they can double or even

66
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:00,906
triple the chance of survival, because

67
00:03:00,906 --> 00:03:02,919
every minute without compressions or a

68
00:03:02,919 --> 00:03:05,359
shock lowers it. The actions of the

69
00:03:05,359 --> 00:03:07,738
nearest person in the first three minutes

70
00:03:07,738 --> 00:03:09,538
matter more than everything done

71
00:03:09,538 --> 00:03:10,558
afterwards.

72
00:03:12,537 --> 00:03:15,892
The 2025 guidelines replace four separate

73
00:03:15,892 --> 00:03:18,221
chains with one Chain of Survival, for

74
00:03:18,221 --> 00:03:21,103
adults and children, in hospital and out.

75
00:03:21,366 --> 00:03:23,365
Six links, in order.

76
00:03:23,628 --> 00:03:25,976
Early recognition and emergency

77
00:03:25,976 --> 00:03:29,247
activation. High-quality cardiopulmonary

78
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:31,885
resuscitation, now including breaths,

79
00:03:31,885 --> 00:03:33,623
especially in children and in

80
00:03:33,623 --> 00:03:35,385
opioid-related arrest.

81
00:03:35,627 --> 00:03:38,518
Defibrillation, a cornerstone of care.

82
00:03:38,801 --> 00:03:40,565
Advanced resuscitation.

83
00:03:40,989 --> 00:03:43,206
Post-cardiac arrest care.

84
00:03:43,469 --> 00:03:45,803
And recovery and survivorship.

85
00:03:46,065 --> 00:03:48,949
The details vary between settings, but the

86
00:03:48,949 --> 00:03:50,296
chain is the same.

87
00:03:52,354 --> 00:03:55,461
If you trained on the 2020 guidelines, you

88
00:03:55,461 --> 00:03:57,762
are updating, not relearning.

89
00:03:57,952 --> 00:04:00,236
Four separate chains became one.

90
00:04:00,476 --> 00:04:03,256
The term rescue breaths is no longer used:

91
00:04:03,256 --> 00:04:05,449
breaths means assisted breathing, and

92
00:04:05,449 --> 00:04:07,828
ventilation is reserved for mechanical

93
00:04:07,828 --> 00:04:11,163
support. The compression rate stays at 100

94
00:04:11,163 --> 00:04:14,499
to 120 a minute, and single-rescuer adult

95
00:04:14,499 --> 00:04:17,244
resuscitation stays at 30 compressions to

96
00:04:17,244 --> 00:04:18,279
2 breaths.

97
00:04:20,254 --> 00:04:23,204
To summarise. Cardiac arrest is an

98
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:25,644
electrical failure that stops the heart's

99
00:04:25,644 --> 00:04:28,048
output, unlike a heart attack, which is a

100
00:04:28,048 --> 00:04:29,712
circulation problem.

101
00:04:29,938 --> 00:04:32,685
Two of its four rhythms can be shocked.

102
00:04:32,885 --> 00:04:35,441
Survival depends on six links, and the

103
00:04:35,441 --> 00:04:37,948
nearest person is the first of them.

104
00:04:38,132 --> 00:04:39,767
This is a knowledge course:

105
00:04:39,767 --> 00:04:42,207
it does not replace hands-on basic life

106
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:44,220
support training with manikin skills

107
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:45,240
assessment.

108
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,261
Keep learning with MedSkills.

